The problem of partitioning an array into two subsets with equal sum is a classic example of the partition problem, which is a well-known NP-complete problem. However, dynamic programming can be used to solve this problem in pseudo-polynomial time, i.e., O(n*S), where n is the length of the input array and S is the sum of all the elements in the array.
The basic idea behind the dynamic programming solution is to create a 2D boolean array dp[i][j], where dp[i][j] is true if there exists a subset of elements from the first i elements of the input array that sum up to j. The algorithm starts by initializing dp[0][0] to true, since there exists an empty subset that sums up to 0. Then, for each element in the input array, we iterate over all possible sums from S/2 to 0 and update the dp array accordingly. Specifically, dp[i][j] is true if either dp[i-1][j] is true (i.e., the ith element is not included in the subset that sums up to j) or dp[i-1][j-arr[i-1]] is true (i.e., the ith element is included in the subset that sums up to j). Finally, we can backtrack through the dp array to find the two subsets that have equal sum.
Here is the implementation of the algorithm in Python:
```python
def partition(arr):
 S = sum(arr)
 if S % 2 != 0:
 return None # no partition possible
 target = S // 2
 n = len(arr)
 dp = [[False for _ in range(target+1)] for _ in range(n+1)]
 for i in range(n+1):
 dp[i][0] = True
 for i in range(1, n+1):
 for j in range(1, target+1):
 if j >= arr[i-1]:
 dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] or dp[i-1][j-arr[i-1]]
 else:
 dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j]
 if not dp[n][target]:
 return None # no partition possible
 subset1, subset2 = [], []
 i, j = n, target
 while i > 0 and j > 0:
 if dp[i-1][j]:
 i -= 1
 elif dp[i-1][j-arr[i-1]]:
 subset1.append(arr[i-1])
 j -= arr[i-1]
 i -= 1
 else:
 break
 subset2 = list(set(arr) - set(subset1))
 return (subset1, subset2)
```
The time complexity of the algorithm is O(n*S), where n is the length of the input array and S is the sum of all the elements in the array. The space complexity is also O(n*S), since we are using a 2D boolean array of size (n+1) x (S/2+1). The correctness of the algorithm can be proved by induction on the size of the input array, using the fact that the problem has optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems, which are the two key properties of dynamic programming.

